#
# prima-materia — chaos magic: Hine, Techniques of Modern Shamanism I-III
#
# The harvest of three works of the corpus of pmt:ChaosMagic, published
# separately as chapbooks and read together in one archive.org collection
# (techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection),
# kept apart from traditions/chaos-magic.ttl, which declares the tradition
# and the works (pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds, pmw:TwoWorldsAndInbetween,
# pmw:TouchedByFire); editions, layer and harvest counts stand as notes on
# the works there. Volume 1: Walking Between the Worlds (Pagan News
# Publications, 1986; on-line version December 1998). Volume 2: Two Worlds
# and Inbetween (Pagan News Publications, chapbook, 1989; on-line version
# June 1999). Volume 3: Touched by Fire (Pagan News Publication, 1989;
# Acrobat conversion March 1998). Pages from the bare number lines at the
# foot of each page; missing ones are placed by sequence and said so.
#
# Harvested under prima-materia#893 with two independent extraction passes
# per volume, reconciled by offset and word overlap, then integrated by a
# reader of the whole volume. Every record was compared with the Hine nodes
# of traditions/chaos-magic-condensed-chaos.ttl,
# traditions/chaos-magic-oven-ready-chaos.ttl,
# traditions/chaos-magic-prime-chaos.ttl,
# traditions/chaos-magic-pseudonomicon.ttl and
# traditions/chaos-magic-aspects-of-evocation.ttl: where an earlier Hine
# node carries the same claim, no node is written here; a node on the same
# topic in another form carries a note beginning "Differs from". Signs of
# success, outcome reports and conditions of failure carry a note beginning
# "Counted as".
#


#
# Volume 1: Walking Between the Worlds
#

#
# Introduction
#

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwWalkingBetweenTheWorlds a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Walking between the worlds: aim of the exercise collection"@en, "Zwischen den Welten gehen: Ziel der Übungssammlung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The book gathers practical exercises meant to sharpen awareness of one's surroundings: first sensory training, then trance states and the group dynamics of helping others into trance. The shamanic outlook is described as heightened environmental awareness plus the ability to mediate between everyday and sacred sides of experience; 'walking between the worlds' means stretching perception from the ordinary to the extraordinary hidden within it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Introduction, p. 4 (no foot number; page from the contents list); read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwExercisesDoNotMakeAShamanCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Exercises do not make one a shaman"@en, "Die Übungen machen niemanden zum Schamanen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author warns that using these techniques will not turn the reader into a shaman or shamanka; they only draw on the shamanic outlook."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwWalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Introduction, p. 4 (no foot number; page from the contents list); read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwExercisesTestedOnTheCourse a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Provenance and testing of the exercises"@en, "Herkunft und Erprobung der Übungen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Hine says that many of the exercises began on the Shamanic Development Course that he and Sheila Broun ran, and that he and others found them useful when first stepping into a magical universe; this is the whole account of their testing that the book gives."@en ;
    skos:note "Added in the reconciliation of the two extraction passes: only the second pass carried it."@en, "The Introduction dates the course to 1987, although the copyright line of the booklet gives 1986."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Introduction, p. 4 (no foot number; page from the contents list), and Basic Exercises, p. 9; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwCourseSurpassedHopesOutcome a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The course surpassed its organisers' hopes"@en, "Der Kurs übertraf die Hoffnungen seiner Leiter"@de ;
    skos:definition "Thanks to the participants' enthusiasm, criticism and own experience, Hine reports, the Shamanic Development Course went far beyond what he and Sheila Broun had hoped for it."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as an outcome report, not as a sign announced in advance: the course is said to have far surpassed its organisers' hopes for its success, owing to the participants' enthusiasm, criticism and experience. No measure of success is given, and the report stands in the acknowledgements."@en, "Added in the reconciliation of the two extraction passes: only the second pass carried it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Introduction, p. 4 (no foot number; page from the contents list); read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .



#
# The Shamanic Survival
#

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwShamanismSourceOfMagicAndReligion a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Shamanism as the source of magic and religion"@en, "Schamanismus als Quelle von Magie und Religion"@de ;
    skos:definition "Hine calls the shaman among the oldest and most lasting figures of human evolution and shamanism the root of both magic and religion, citing Mircea Eliade's description of it as an archaic technique for ecstasy. Its structural features are said to reach back to the Upper Palaeolithic and to stay alike across cultures and periods, the surface details varying while the principles meet a need of the psyche that has not changed over many hundred thousand years."@en ;
    skos:note "Added in the reconciliation of the two extraction passes: only the second pass carried it."@en, "Mircea Eliade is quoted for the phrase only; his work is not cited by title."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), The Shamanic Survival, p. 5; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwShamanShamankaTerm a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Term shaman / shamanka"@en, "Begriff Schamane / Schamanka"@de ;
    skos:definition "The word shaman is derived from the Tungusic root saman of Siberia; shaman is used for a male practitioner and shamanka for a female one."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), The Shamanic Survival, The Shamanic World-View, pp. 6-7 (no foot number survives for p. 6); read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwMultiLayeredCosmosAxisMundi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Multi-layered shamanic cosmos and axis mundi"@en, "Vielschichtiger schamanischer Kosmos und Weltachse"@de ;
    skos:definition "As a loose composite, the shaman's universe has an upper heavenly world and a lower earth-deep world, each with spirit rulers and other inhabitants, plus rulers of the quarters; the levels are joined by a central axis (sky ladder or world-tree) through which the shaman enters every level."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicCondBAxisMundi (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 node makes the axis a way of placing oneself at the centre of every state of consciousness; here it belongs to a composite of the tribal world-view, a cosmos of upper and lower worlds with their spirit rulers, whose levels the shaman reaches by the central axis."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), The Shamanic Survival, The Shamanic World-View, pp. 6-7 (no foot number survives for p. 6); read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwSpiritRulersNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Spirit rulers of the levels and quarters"@en, "Geisterherrscher der Ebenen und Himmelsrichtungen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Beings said, in the composite shamanic world-view, to rule the overworld, the underworld and the principal directions, alongside other denizens of those levels."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "spirit rulers of the overworld, the underworld and the quarters" ;
    pm:nameRole "rulers of the levels of the cosmos and of the principal directions" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), The Shamanic Survival, The Shamanic World-View, pp. 6-7 (no foot number survives for p. 6); read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwEqualityOfLifeMetamorphosis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Equality of all life and metamorphosis"@en, "Gleichheit allen Lebens und Gestaltwandel"@de ;
    skos:definition "The shaman is said to be at one with nature, humans and animals being equal, expressed e.g. in rites appeasing the souls of slain animals (skulls set facing east, the place of rebirth). Related is metamorphosis: humans and animals can take each other's shape; many origin stories say all once had this power, lost through breaking a taboo, so that it became the shamanka's sole domain."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), The Shamanic Survival, The Shamanic World-View, pp. 6-7 (no foot number survives for p. 6); read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwJaguarNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Jaguar"@en, "Jaguar"@de ;
    skos:definition "In South America the jaguar is called the most common double of the shaman, so closely that the terms for the animal and for the shaman are used interchangeably."@en ;
    skos:note "Taken up again under Shape-Shifting, where some Central American tribes are said to use one word for both (pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwFavouriteShamanicForms)."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Jaguar" ;
    pm:nameRole "the shaman's animal double in South America" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), The Shamanic Survival, The Shamanic World-View, pp. 6-7 (no foot number survives for p. 6); read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwGatewayBetweenWorlds a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Gateway between the worlds"@en, "Tor zwischen den Welten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Central to the shamanic vision is a gateway into otherworlds inhabited by ancestral spirits and demons, often pictured as clashing rocks, a monster's jaws or icebergs."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), The Shamanic Survival, The Shamanic World-View, pp. 6-7 (no foot number survives for p. 6); read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwPsychotropicOriginsOfShamanism a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Psychotropic origin hypothesis and routes to ecstasy"@en, "Psychotrope Ursprungshypothese und Wege zur Ekstase"@de ;
    skos:definition "Hine reports the ethnobiologist R.G. Wasson as proposing that the great world religions arose from psychotropic experience, and R.K. Seigal, an American researcher, as linking shamans' drug visions to the designs of tribal pottery and weaving; most shamanic cultures are said to have a psychotropic plant of their own (fly agaric in Siberia, mescal bean and peyote among American Indian shamans, at least 80 kinds in the Americas). He adds that drugs are only one road to ecstasy beside drumming, dance, song, fasting, going without sleep and feats of endurance."@en ;
    skos:note "Wasson and Seigal are named without titles; the node holds Hine's ascription."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), The Shamanic Survival, The Origins of Shamanism, pp. 6-7 (no foot number survives for p. 6); read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwSomaNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Soma"@en, "Soma"@de ;
    skos:definition "The sacred Soma of the Vedic hymns is said to have been tentatively identified with the Amanita muscaria mushroom used by Siberian shamans."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Soma" ;
    pm:nameRole "divine drink of the Rig-Veda, tentatively identified with the fly agaric of the Siberian shamans" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), The Shamanic Survival, The Origins of Shamanism, pp. 6-7 (no foot number survives for p. 6); read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwBrokenLineagesAdoptedSymbolSystems a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Broken lineages and freely adopted symbol systems"@en, "Abgerissene Traditionslinien und frei gewählte Symbolsysteme"@de ;
    skos:definition "Since all lines of tradition are broken, Westerners can enter any world-view and imprint its symbols on the mind; the criterion offered for choosing a belief system is that it should inflame one's imagination. This contrasts with the change-averse hunter-gatherer society the shaman came from."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicCondCAnyBeliefSystem (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 node says that magic needs a belief system but that any one serves if it excites the magician; here the choice is set in a history, every line of tradition being broken so that a Westerner may step into any world-view at will, against the change-averse hunter-gatherer society the shaman came from."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), The Shamanic Survival, Shamanic Traditions, p. 8; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwDeepMindSpeaksAdoptedSymbols a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Deep mind speaking through an adopted symbol set"@en, "Tiefe Psyche spricht in einem angenommenen Symbolsatz"@de ;
    skos:definition "If one works with a chosen world-view long enough, the deeper layers of the mind are said to begin communicating in that system's symbols."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicCondBDeepMindCommunicatingThroughSymbols (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 node has the Deep Mind give insight dressed in the symbols it has absorbed; here the claim is conditional on long work inside one adopted world-view, after which the deeper mind answers in that system's set of symbols."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), The Shamanic Survival, Shamanic Traditions, p. 8; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwChangingOneselfAsChangingSociety a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Changing oneself as changing society"@en, "Selbstveränderung als Gesellschaftsveränderung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The modern idea of spiritual progress is said to equate changing oneself with changing society as a whole, the direct opposite of the hunter-gatherer outlook, which meets any change threatening the continuity of tradition with deep suspicion; together with broken lineages this is named the problem facing anyone who would emulate the shaman today."@en ;
    skos:note "Added by the integrator from the reading of the whole text; no extraction record carries it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), The Shamanic Survival, Shamanic Traditions, p. 8; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwValueOfShamanicTechniques a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Value of shamanic techniques today"@en, "Wert schamanischer Techniken heute"@de ;
    skos:definition "Their value is placed in drawing knowledge up from inside oneself and in relating with others: ecstasy can move from the private back to the communal sphere, and techniques that take us back to our ancestors can restore a sense of community."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), The Shamanic Survival, The Value of Shamanism, p. 8; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwCommunityBeyondCulturalDifference a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sense of community beyond cultural difference"@en, "Gemeinschaftsgefühl jenseits kultureller Unterschiede"@de ;
    skos:definition "Regaining the spirit of the collective past is said to give a feeling of community with our forebears that transcends all cultural differences."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), The Shamanic Survival, The Value of Shamanism, p. 8; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwReconnectionAndAdaptation a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Reconnection with self and easier adaptation"@en, "Wiederverbindung mit sich und leichtere Anpassung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Coming to perceive the world in the shamanic manner is said to reconnect us with ourselves and let us adapt far more easily to our surroundings."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), The Shamanic Survival, The Value of Shamanism, p. 8; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .



#
# Basic Exercises
#

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwGlamourHardWorkLongDurationCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Glamour, hard work and long duration"@en, "Glanz, harte Arbeit und lange Dauer"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author stresses that shamanic techniques carry a glamour, that the work is very hard, and that unless one is free of all other commitments it will take much longer than expected."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicCondAWeekendWorkshopCautela (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 node warns that instant courses mislead and that learning magic is like learning to drive; here the warning is against the glamour of shamanic techniques, with the stress that the work is very hard and, for anyone with other commitments, slower than expected."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwWalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Basic Exercises, p. 9; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwFiveEssentialsShamanicApproach a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "Five essentials of the shamanic approach"@en, "Fünf Grundelemente des schamanischen Ansatzes"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author's own list of essentials: (1) enhanced senses; (2) awareness and understanding of one's own BodyMind; (3) self-understanding; (4) empathy; (5) a wish to engage with others, whether people or spirits."@en ;
    skos:note "Hine's own list (pm:Systematizing), numbered by him; the first essential is entered as pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwEnhancedSenses."@en ;
    pm:compiledBy "Phil Hine, in Walking Between the Worlds" ;
    dcterms:date "1986"^^xsd:gYear ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Basic Exercises, p. 9; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwEnhancedSenses a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Enhanced senses"@en, "Geschärfte Sinne"@de ;
    skos:definition "Sharpened sensory perception, the first essential of the shamanic approach, which the sensory exercises (sight, sound, smell, space, speech, touch) aim to cultivate by breaking unconscious perceptual habits."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Basic Exercises, p. 9; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwLearningAmongOthersNotSecretSchools a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Against the Western 'secret school' model of solitary learning"@en, "Gegen das westliche Geheimschulmodell des Einzellernens"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author sets the shaman, who does not learn apart from others, against the Western-Mediterranean 'secret schools' tradition; he also dismisses fancy degrees and titles earned inside a system in favour of demonstrable competence in the wider world."@en ;
    skos:note "Only Hine's side is read; the secret-school model is named, not cited from any work."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Basic Exercises, p. 9; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwCommunityFeedbackPrerequisite a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Community and outside feedback before shamanic learning"@en, "Gemeinschaft und Rückmeldung vor schamanischem Lernen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Shamanic learning is held to require a community, however small or scattered, from which one gets feedback, advice and criticism; many techniques need group exploration since growth in total isolation is said to be impossible."@en ;
    skos:note "The Introduction states the same ground: growth is held impossible in total isolation from others."@en, "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicCondBNoMagicianIsolation (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 node, from the trance chapter, says no one becomes a magician cut off from others and that teachers are scarce; here, in the basic exercises, the need is for a community that gives feedback, advice and criticism, since a title one gives oneself has nothing to check it."@en ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwLearningAmongOthersNotSecretSchools ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwWalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Without other people's opinions a person may claim to be anything at all, and nothing outside the person checks the claim." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Basic Exercises, p. 9; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwCompetenceOverSystem a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Competence over system"@en, "Kompetenz vor System"@de ;
    skos:definition "What counts is said to be competence, the ability to deliver, which in modern society may range from blessing houses and officiating at funerals and weddings to helping someone through a benefits claim; which system one follows is said not to matter as long as the results are relevant to oneself and meaningful to clients."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicCondAGoodMagician (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 node defines the good magician as effective and adaptable across life; here competence is the ability to deliver services to others (blessing houses, funerals, weddings, a benefits claim), and the system followed is said not to matter so long as its fruits mean something to oneself and to clients."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Basic Exercises, p. 9; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwBreakingSensoryBodyMindHabits a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Aim of practice: breaking sensory and BodyMind habits"@en, "Ziel der Übung: Sinnes- und Körpergeist-Gewohnheiten brechen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The exercises aim to break unconsciously formed habits of sense and BodyMind, widening perception beyond the ordinary toward the extra-ordinary; this needs determination, tenacity and sometimes sheer stubbornness."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicCondABloodyMindedness (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 node has determination, tenacity and stubbornness grow out of persisting with a practice; here they are what the practice of breaking sensory and BodyMind habits needs from the start."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Basic Exercises, A Note on Practice, p. 10; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .



#
# Sight Exercises
#

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwOverlookedSurroundings a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "We fail to notice our surroundings"@en, "Wir übersehen unsere Umgebung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Sight dominates perception, yet much around us goes unnoticed; we filter out more than we admit and miss subtle changes, though such subtle cues are said to matter most for magical development."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Sight Exercises, p. 12; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmp:ChaosMagicTmsWbwWalkingARoute a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Walking a Route"@en, "Eine Strecke gehen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Take a route you walk so often that you no longer notice its details; this time stay intensely aware of everything along the way, and afterwards write down all you noticed."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Sight Exercises, Walking a Route, p. 12; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmp:ChaosMagicTmsWbwWalkingARouteGroupVariant a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Walking a Route, group variant"@en, "Eine Strecke gehen, Gruppenvariante"@de ;
    skos:definition "Lead a group from one place to another and ask each member to list all they observed of the stretch between."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Sight Exercises, Walking a Route, p. 12; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmp:ChaosMagicTmsWbwKimsGame a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Kim's Game"@en, "Kims Spiel"@de ;
    skos:definition "Put several objects on a tray, look at them for about a minute, cover them with a cloth, then see how many you can name in 30 seconds; try it with known as well as strange objects."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Sight Exercises, Kim's Game, p. 12; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmp:ChaosMagicTmsWbwColourCensus a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Colour Census"@en, "Farbzählung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Pick a colour for a day or a week; stay aware of where it turns up around you and whether it colours your moods and thinking, and possibly the moods of those nearby."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Sight Exercises, Colour Census, p. 12; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwNoticingVisualDetail a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Noticing visual detail and subtle change"@en, "Wahrnehmung visueller Details und feiner Veränderungen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Heightened visual attention to the details and subtle shifts of one's environment, which such exercises are said to train by exposing our habitual laziness in seeing."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Sight Exercises, p. 13; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

