#
# Status Shifts
#

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwShamansRolesAndStatusShifts a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Shaman's many roles and status shifts"@en, "Die vielen Rollen des Schamanen und Statuswechsel"@de ;
    skos:definition "The shaman is said to take many roles (trickster, sacred clown, healer, mediator, sorcerer), and to assume them takes confidence, insight, empathy and an acquired skill in presenting oneself, approached through status shifts. Status lies in stance, tone, words, eye contact and gesture; we shift it all the time, raising or lowering ourselves in talk (Stephen Potter's 'OneUpManship'), and we ascribe status to others by expectation, as when the author in a white coat was taken for a doctor. Some deflect projected high status, as Zen masters do with humour or nonsense, and the sacred lowering and raising of status is common across cultures: the king who swaps with a beggar, the god come to earth disguised as a mortal, the fool-king honoured for a day and then sacrificed."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicPrcBStatusShiftsInvocation (Prime Chaos, 1999): the 1999 node borrows the low-high status scale from drama for invocation, from supplicant to the deity's own status; here status shifts are the way into the shaman's many social roles, with everyday one-upmanship, ascribed status and the sacred inversion of status in myth."@en, "The sentences on Zen masters and on the sacred inversion of status were carried by the second extraction pass only and are folded into this node."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Status Shifts, p. 25; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwAdroitStatusShifting a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Adroit status shifting"@en, "Gewandter Statuswechsel"@de ;
    skos:definition "Skill at shifting one's status to fit the situation, called useful for the shamanic practitioner."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Status Shifts, Gurus and Disciples, p. 27; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwGurusDisciplesYieldsAdroitStatusShifting a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Gurus and Disciples trains status shifting"@en, "Gurus und Jünger schult den Statuswechsel"@de ;
    skos:definition "The two-player game, with roles swapped and optionally in gibberish, is given to demonstrate status shifts and our collusion in them, on the way to the skill of shifting status to suit the situation."@en ;
    skos:note "The game itself is carried by pmp:ChaosMagicPrcCGurusDisciplesStatusGame (Prime Chaos, 1999); this node holds only the 1986 link to the skill, which the text makes by sequence, not by a stated effect."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicPrcCGurusDisciplesStatusGame ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwAdroitStatusShifting ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Status Shifts, Gurus and Disciples, p. 27; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwStuckInOneSafeStatusCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Getting stuck in one safe status"@en, "Festfahren in einem sicheren Status"@de ;
    skos:definition "We tend to settle on one status projection and manoeuvre into situations that let us keep it and so feel secure."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwAdroitStatusShifting ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Status Shifts, Gurus and Disciples, p. 27; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwModernShamanStatusFromCompetence a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Status of the modern shaman rests on competence"@en, "Status des modernen Schamanen beruht auf Kompetenz"@de ;
    skos:definition "Unlike a shamanka among her own people, who is automatically granted high standing, a self-declared shaman here gains none automatically; it depends on the situation, self-projection and being good at the work. Truly competent practitioners are said to be granted high status by clients, and much of practical shamanism rests on personal charisma. Briefly upsetting the tribe's status conventions belongs to the shaman's task; the nearest modern figures are comedians and street performers."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicPrcCHighAccordedStatusEasesTrance (Prime Chaos, 1999): the 1999 node says that the higher the status granted, the more readily a magician puts others in trance or heals them; here the point is that in our culture a self-declared shaman gets no status automatically, only from the situation, self-projection and being good at the work."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Status Shifts, Gurus and Disciples, p. 27; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwClientsWithoutFaithFailure a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Clients without faith make the work harder"@en, "Klienten ohne Vertrauen erschweren die Arbeit"@de ;
    skos:definition "If clients have little faith in one's ability to help them, working with them is said to be more difficult."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: of work with clients who have little faith in the practitioner's ability to help them; the work is said to become more difficult. No deadline or rate; the cause is put on the client's lack of faith, which the text ties to the status a practitioner earns by competence."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Status Shifts, Gurus and Disciples, p. 27; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmp:ChaosMagicTmsWbwShiftingLeaderStatusForMaskWork a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Shifting leader status for mask-work"@en, "Leiterstatus für Maskenarbeit wechseln"@de ;
    skos:definition "When running a workshop on masks, the author deliberately dropped his usual non-directive style: he adopted a graver bearing, stood rather than sat and behaved so as to command attention, then returned to a non-directive manner once the workshop ended."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Status Shifts, Gurus and Disciples, p. 27; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwMaskWorkNearPossessionCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Mask-work can verge on possession"@en, "Maskenarbeit kann an Besessenheit grenzen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Teaching mask-work can lead people into trance states close to possession; non-directive leadership, though fashionable, is not always appropriate."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicCondCMaskPossession ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Status Shifts, Gurus and Disciples, p. 28; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwDirectiveAnchorForMaskTrancePrerequisite a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "A directive leader as anchor for mask trance"@en, "Ein führender Leiter als Anker für Maskentrance"@de ;
    skos:definition "Group trance work bordering on possession is said definitely to need a leader acting as an anchor, so that members dare to let go knowing someone is in control."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicPrcCHighAccordedStatusEasesTrance (Prime Chaos, 1999): the 1999 node says a high-status director lets members let go in intense ritual; here, from Hine's own mask workshop, a directive leader is said to be definitely required as anchor when the trance borders on possession."@en ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicTmsWbwShiftingLeaderStatusForMaskWork ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicCondCMaskPossession ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Without someone in control as anchor, members lack the confidence to let go into trance bordering on possession, and the situation may get out of hand." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Status Shifts, Gurus and Disciples, p. 28; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwRelaxedToSeriousEasier a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Relaxed-to-serious is easier than the reverse"@en, "Von locker zu ernst ist leichter als umgekehrt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author observes that moving from a relaxed to a serious manner is easier than the opposite, and sees shamans in films of tribal ceremonies alternate between indulgence and tight control."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicPrcBMasterCeremoniesIndulgentHighStatus (Prime Chaos, 1999): the 1999 node reports Keith Johnstone on Voudou priests who keep high status while indulging the possessed; here Hine, from his own workshops, finds it easier to move from relaxed to serious than back, and sees shamans in films of tribal ceremonies alternate indulgence and a tight rein."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Status Shifts, Gurus and Disciples, p. 28; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .



#
# Jack Your Body...
#

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwDangerousTranceRoutesCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Danger of certain trance routes"@en, "Manche Wege in die Trance sind gefährlich"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author disclaims: certain ways into trance are dangerous, and anyone trying them bears sole responsibility."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwFourteenRoutesIntoTrance ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., p. 29; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwRestrainingEntrancedPersonCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Restraining an entranced person can hinder her"@en, "Das Festhalten einer Person in Trance kann sie behindern"@de ;
    skos:definition "A 'sensitive' woman entering trance in a circle began to thrash and scream; members tried to restrain her, but she was in fact fighting a demon in her trance and their interventions obstructed her."@en ;
    skos:note "An incident Hine watched from within the circle, years before writing."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicCondBTranceDeliberateAlterationPerception ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., What is Trance?, p. 29; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwForciblyRecalledFromTranceCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Being forcibly brought out of trance"@en, "Gewaltsam aus der Trance geholt werden"@de ;
    skos:definition "After a botched ritual the author did a shape-shifting exercise in a quiet room and went into a fairly deep trance; onlookers concluded that demons were assailing him astrally and forcibly pulled him out, which he found unpleasant. Moral: when trance falls outside expected behaviour, others easily do the wrong thing."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicCondBTranceDeliberateAlterationPerception ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., What is Trance?, p. 30; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwShadesOfTrance a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Shades of trance and no fixed normal state"@en, "Abstufungen der Trance, kein fester Normalzustand"@de ;
    skos:definition "'Normal' consciousness is hard to pin down; we constantly move between grades of trance, ranging from reverie to the complete absorption of vigorous exercise, and both reverie and exertion are said to be routes into shamanic trance."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicCondBTranceDeliberateAlterationPerception (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 node defines trance as deliberately altering perception to set parameters; here the stress is that normal consciousness can hardly be tracked down, that we drift through shades of trance all day, and that daydreaming and physical exertion are both routes into shamanic trance."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., What is Trance?, p. 30; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwFourteenRoutesIntoTrance a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "Fourteen routes into trance"@en, "Vierzehn Wege in die Trance"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author's table of routes: dancing, gyrating, drumming, singing, chanting, hyperventilating, pleasure, gazing, fasting, sleep deprivation, sensory deprivation, sensory overload, emotional arousal, pain. Shamanic group rituals are called well-orchestrated combinations of many of them."@en ;
    skos:note "Hine's own table (pm:Systematizing), printed in two columns and read here as fourteen entries."@en, "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicCondBExcitatoryInhibitoryTranceRoutes (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 node sorts routes into excitatory and inhibitory; here the routes stand in an unsorted list of fourteen, any of which, pursued relentlessly, is said to lead into trance, and group rituals are called orchestrated uses of many of them."@en ;
    pm:compiledBy "Phil Hine, in Walking Between the Worlds" ;
    dcterms:date "1986"^^xsd:gYear ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., What is Trance?, p. 31; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmp:ChaosMagicTmsWbwPursuingARouteRelentlessly a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Pursuing a route into trance relentlessly"@en, "Einen Tranceweg unnachgiebig verfolgen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Choose any one of the listed routes and pursue it relentlessly with determination and practice; push yourself over the edge again and again."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., What is Trance?, p. 31; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwPursuingARouteYieldsTranceAtWill a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Pursuing a route relentlessly yields entry into trance"@en, "Einen Tranceweg unnachgiebig verfolgen führt in die Trance"@de ;
    skos:definition "Any of the listed routes, pursued with determination and practice, is said to bring one into trance states."@en ;
    skos:note "The capacity is the one already entered from Condensed Chaos."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicTmsWbwPursuingARouteRelentlessly ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicCondBEnteringProlongingTranceWill ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., What is Trance?, p. 31; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwRepeatedlyOverTheEdgePrerequisite a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Repeatedly going over the edge before trance stability"@en, "Wiederholtes Überschreiten der Schwelle vor stabiler Trance"@de ;
    skos:definition "Only by pushing oneself over the edge many times does one learn to override the reflex of snapping back out once one senses control slipping."@en ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicTmsWbwPursuingARouteRelentlessly ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwOverridingTheReflexToLeaveTrance ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Without pushing oneself over the edge again and again, one keeps jerking out of trance as soon as control is felt to slip." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., What is Trance?, p. 31; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwOverridingTheReflexToLeaveTrance a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Overriding the reflex to leave trance"@en, "Den Reflex zum Verlassen der Trance überwinden"@de ;
    skos:definition "Staying in trance when one feels control slipping, rather than jerking out."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., What is Trance?, p. 31; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwTranceIsMeansNotEnd a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Trance is means, not end"@en, "Trance ist Mittel, nicht Zweck"@de ;
    skos:definition "Trance should serve an objective, such as entering an innerworld, being possessed by a spirit, or exercising will. Cultures can be characterised by their dominant route: Plains nomads used pain (also showing prowess), the Kaula shamans of India prolonged sexual ecstasy; drugs are deferred to a later discussion."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., What is Trance?, p. 31; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmp:ChaosMagicTmsWbwPersonalDreamLanguage a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Developing a personal dream-language"@en, "Eine persönliche Traumsprache entwickeln"@de ;
    skos:definition "Rather than adopting a fixed system of dream interpretation, build your own dream-language or avoid rigid schemes altogether; called more rewarding."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., Dreams, p. 35; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwClavesSpiritsReceiving a pm:Receiving ;
    rdfs:label "Spirits of the claves dictate their names"@en, "Die Geister der Klanghölzer diktieren ihre Namen"@de ;
    skos:definition "After making a pair of claves for a musical improvisation session (and getting very drunk), the author dreamed that two spirits introduced themselves as the spirits of his claves, gave him their names to brand into the wood using a heated wire, and asked to be fed butter."@en ;
    pm:receivedBy "Phil Hine" ;
    pm:claimedSpeaker "Two spirits who called themselves the spirits of his claves" ;
    pm:receptionCircumstance "A clear dream on the evening after he had made a set of claves for an improvised music session of the Shamanic Development Course, having got very drunk at the pub; the spirits gave their names for him to brand into the wood with a heated wire and asked to be given butter to eat." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., Dreams, p. 35; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwClavesSpiritsNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Spirits of the claves"@en, "Geister der Klanghölzer"@de ;
    skos:definition "Two spirits who in the author's dream claimed to belong to the claves he had made, gave their names, and asked to be fed butter."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "the spirits of the claves" ;
    pm:nameRole "indwelling spirits of a pair of ritual musical instruments" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., Dreams, p. 35; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmp:ChaosMagicTmsWbwEnsoulingClavesWithDreamNames a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Ensouling an instrument with dream-given names"@en, "Ein Instrument mit traumgegebenen Namen beseelen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Following the dream, the author burned the spirits' names into the claves with a hot wire and fed them butter."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., Dreams, p. 35; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwClavesBecameMoreThanWoodOutcome a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The claves became more than wood"@en, "Die Klanghölzer wurden mehr als Holz"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author reports that this made the claves something more than two sawn-off pieces of broom handle."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as an outcome report, not as a sign announced in advance: after the names were burnt in and the butter given, the claves became something more than two pieces of broom handle. Hine offers the dream to illustrate how prolonged magical activity shapes dreams; what the more consists in is not said."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., Dreams, p. 35; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwDreamDiaryBeforeDreamLanguagePrerequisite a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Dream diary before serious dream work"@en, "Traumtagebuch vor ernsthafter Traumarbeit"@de ;
    skos:definition "For anyone taking up dream-life seriously a dream diary is called essential: writing dreams down helps one remember them and is indispensable once one starts to evolve a dream-language of one's own."@en ;
    skos:note "Called essential and indispensable, but no consequence of leaving it out is given."@en, "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicCondBDreamDiaryFirstStepDreamPrerequisite (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 node names the dream record the first step of dream magic; here it is the condition of a personal dream-language."@en ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicCondBKeepingDreamDiary ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicTmsWbwPersonalDreamLanguage ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., Dreams, p. 35; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwDreamMantraEmptiesIntent a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Deep Mind and sigil mechanism"@en, "Tiefer Geist und Wirkweise des Sigills"@de ;
    skos:definition "Constant repetition is said eventually to empty the mind of the linked intent and to prompt the Deep Mind (the subconscious) to produce the fitting dream images."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., Dreams, Dream Control, p. 36; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmp:ChaosMagicTmsWbwGroupDreams a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Group Dreams"@en, "Gruppenträume"@de ;
    skos:definition "Several people try to dream of being together in some actual or imagined place familiar to all, helped by visualisation and mantra; first they aim to perceive the dream in common, later at performing willed acts in it. The author's group once tried this using M.C. Escher's print 'Relativity'."@en ;
    skos:note "No outcome is given for the attempt with the Escher print."@en, "Differs from pmp:ChaosMagicCondBOrchestratingGroupSharedDreams (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 practice sets up the shared setting with a semi-structured pathworking; here the group picks a real or imaginary place all know, uses visualisation and mantra, and aims first at perceiving the dream in common and only then at willed acts in it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., Dreams, Group Dreams, p. 36; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwGroupDreamsYieldSharedPerception a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Group dreaming aims at a shared dream"@en, "Gruppenträumen zielt auf einen gemeinsamen Traum"@de ;
    skos:definition "The group attempt, helped by visualisation and mantra, is directed at perceiving the dream in common."@en ;
    skos:note "Stated as an attempt; the one attempt reported has no outcome."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicTmsWbwGroupDreams ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwSharedGroupDreamPerception ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., Dreams, Group Dreams, p. 36; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwCommonPerceptionBeforeWilledActsPrerequisite a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Shared dream perception before willed acts"@en, "Gemeinsame Traumwahrnehmung vor gewollten Handlungen"@de ;
    skos:definition "In group dreaming the group must first perceive the dream jointly; deliberate actions follow afterwards."@en ;
    skos:note "Named as first and later step; no consequence of skipping is given."@en ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwSharedGroupDreamPerception ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwWilledActsInGroupDream ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., Dreams, Group Dreams, p. 36; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwWilledActsInGroupDream a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Willed acts in a group dream"@en, "Gewollte Handlungen im Gruppentraum"@de ;
    skos:definition "Performing intended actions together inside a dream that a group already perceives in common."@en ;
    skos:note "Written as the later step named under Group Dreams; the text gives no example."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., Dreams, Group Dreams, p. 36; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwSharedGroupDreamPerception a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Common perception of a group dream"@en, "Gemeinsame Wahrnehmung eines Gruppentraums"@de ;
    skos:definition "Several people perceiving themselves together in one dream place, real or imaginary, known to all of them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., Dreams, Group Dreams, p. 36; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwLucidDreaming a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Lucid dreaming"@en, "Klarträumen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Being aware within a dream that one is dreaming; such dreams are said to be far easier to remember and to allow intentional action instead of passive participation."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicCondBLucidityTriggers (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 node defines lucidity as realising one dreams so that the dream can be changed, triggered by incongruity or a recurring dream; here lucid dreams are also said to be much easier to remember and to let one act with intent rather than drift."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., Dreams, Lucid Dreams, p. 36; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmp:ChaosMagicTmsWbwLookingForHandsInDreams a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Lucid dreams: looking for one's hands"@en, "Klarträume: nach den eigenen Händen suchen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Make an effort to look at your own hands while dreaming; the jolt of doing so is said to let you enter the lucid state."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., Dreams, Lucid Dreams, p. 36; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwLookingForHandsYieldsLucidity a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Looking for one's hands yields lucidity"@en, "Nach den eigenen Händen suchen führt zur Klarheit"@de ;
    skos:definition "The shock of seeing one's hands in a dream is said to let one enter the lucid state."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicTmsWbwLookingForHandsInDreams ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwLucidDreaming ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., Dreams, Lucid Dreams, p. 36; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwGothBootsLucidityOutcome a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Incongruity triggers lucidity and innerworld entry"@en, "Unstimmigkeit löst Klarheit und Eintritt in die Innenwelt aus"@de ;
    skos:definition "Dreaming of his home town as it was ten years earlier, the author noticed he was wearing his goth boots; the mismatch made the dream lucid, and by projecting a symbol onto a nearby doorway he entered one of the Innerworlds."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as an outcome report, not as a sign announced in advance: Hine became lucid in a dream of his home town as it had been ten years earlier on noticing he wore his goth boots, and then entered one of the Innerworlds through a symbol projected on a doorway. A single first-person case, given as illustration."@en, "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicCondBLucidityTriggers (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 node mentions an incongruity of shoes as a trigger of lucidity; here the report goes on to the use of the lucid state, a symbol projected on a nearby doorway as entry into an Innerworld."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., Dreams, Lucid Dreams, p. 36; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmp:ChaosMagicTmsWbwSymbolOnDoorwayInLucidDream a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Projecting a symbol onto a doorway in a lucid dream"@en, "Ein Symbol im Klartraum auf eine Tür projizieren"@de ;
    skos:definition "Once lucid in a dream, project a symbol onto a doorway in the dream scene and pass through it into one of the Innerworlds."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., Dreams, Lucid Dreams, p. 36; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwDoorwaySymbolYieldsInnerworldEntry a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "A projected symbol opens an Innerworld from a dream"@en, "Ein projiziertes Symbol öffnet aus dem Traum eine Innenwelt"@de ;
    skos:definition "In the author's report, projecting a symbol on a doorway in a lucid dream let him enter one of the Innerworlds."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicTmsWbwSymbolOnDoorwayInLucidDream ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwInnerworldEntryFromDream ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., Dreams, Lucid Dreams, p. 36; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwInnerworldEntryFromDream a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Entering an innerworld from a dream"@en, "Aus dem Traum in eine Innenwelt eintreten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Using lucid dreaming as a passage into one of the Innerworlds."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., Dreams, Lucid Dreams, p. 36; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmp:ChaosMagicTmsWbwAnimalShapeShifting a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Shape-shifting into an animal"@en, "Gestaltwandel in ein Tier"@de ;
    skos:definition "Assume an animal's shape to acquire, for a while, an ability or quality linked with it; the shaman supports this with animal skins, masks, drugs, and imitation of the animal's posture and voice (much as some sports psychologists have athletes visualise themselves as an animal in warm-up)."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmp:ChaosMagicPrcBShapeShiftingAnimalIdentification (Prime Chaos, 1999): the 1999 practice becomes an animal by close observation and identification, with trance depth set by abandonment; here the shaman takes the animal's form to gain its quality for a time, aided by skins, masks, drugs and mimicry, compared to athletes who picture themselves as an animal while warming up."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., Shape-Shifting, p. 37; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwShapeShiftingYieldsAnimalQuality a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Shape-shifting yields an animal's quality for a time"@en, "Gestaltwandel verleiht zeitweilig die Eigenschaft eines Tieres"@de ;
    skos:definition "Taking on an animal's form is said to give, temporarily, an ability or quality linked with it."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicTmsWbwAnimalShapeShifting ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwTemporaryAnimalQuality ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., Shape-Shifting, p. 37; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwTemporaryAnimalQuality a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Temporary animal ability or quality"@en, "Vorübergehende tierische Fähigkeit oder Eigenschaft"@de ;
    skos:definition "A power or trait linked to a given animal, held for a time by assuming its shape."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., Shape-Shifting, p. 37; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmp:ChaosMagicTmsWbwAssumingTheGodForms a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Assuming the God-forms (Golden Dawn)"@en, "Annahme der Gottformen (Golden Dawn)"@de ;
    skos:definition "Hine reports that the Golden Dawn, the Hermetic Order of that name, called the exercise Assuming the God-forms: with various props the practitioner takes on the form of a chosen mythic figure and unites with it until its power is felt as one's own."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmp:ChaosMagicCondCAssumptionOfGodForms (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 practice gives Hine's own procedure of visualising the god behind one and letting it step into the body; here the Golden Dawn exercise is named, by report and without procedure, as the magical counterpart of shamanic shape-shifting."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., Shape-Shifting, p. 37; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwPowerOfMythicFigure a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Taking on the power of a mythic figure"@en, "Die Kraft einer mythischen Gestalt annehmen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Acquiring the power linked to a mythic figure by uniting with its assumed form."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., Shape-Shifting, p. 37; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwGodFormsYieldMythicPower a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Assuming a god-form yields its power"@en, "Gottform annehmen verleiht ihre Kraft"@de ;
    skos:definition "Uniting with an assumed mythic form is said to go on until the figure's power becomes one's own."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicTmsWbwAssumingTheGodForms ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwPowerOfMythicFigure ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
