pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwFavouriteShamanicForms a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Favourite forms of shamans"@en, "Bevorzugte Gestalten von Schamanen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Shamans tend to favour particular forms: in some Central American tribes shaman and jaguar share one word; Jivaro shamans are said to take the form of giant tarantulas; Eastern martial arts have animal forms such as Crane, Serpent and Panther."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., Shape-Shifting, p. 37; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmp:ChaosMagicTmsWbwShapeShiftedVisionJourneys a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Shape-shifted vision journeys"@en, "Gestaltgewandelte Visionsreisen"@de ;
    skos:definition "For innerworld exploration in trance vision or dream, cast your vision out in the form of an animal, for instance a cat stalking the city streets at night."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmp:ChaosMagicPrcCAstralShapeShifting (Prime Chaos, 1999): the 1999 practice takes zoomorphic astral forms to enter astral spaces and meet animal-headed deities; here the start is an everyday animal sent out in trance vision or dream, with a stated progression toward stranger forms."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., Shape-Shifting, p. 37; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmp:ChaosMagicTmsWbwShapeShiftingIntoCompositeEntities a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Shape-shifting into mythological and composite beings"@en, "Gestaltwandel in mythische und zusammengesetzte Wesen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Take on, in vision or dream, the forms of mythological creatures and bizarre composite entities."@en ;
    skos:note "Written as the later step named under Shape-Shifting; the text gives no example."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., Shape-Shifting, p. 37; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwEverydayAnimalsBeforeCompositesPrerequisite a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Everyday animals before mythic composites"@en, "Alltägliche Tiere vor mythischen Mischwesen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Shape-shifting work is to progress from ordinary animals and pets toward mythological and strange composite entities."@en ;
    skos:note "A direction of work without a stated consequence of skipping."@en ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicTmsWbwShapeShiftedVisionJourneys ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicTmsWbwShapeShiftingIntoCompositeEntities ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., Shape-Shifting, p. 37; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwSpiderPhobiaCuredOutcome a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Spider phobia cured through a power animal"@en, "Spinnenphobie durch ein Krafttier geheilt"@de ;
    skos:definition "A friend is reported to have cured her spider phobia by deliberately adopting the spider as her own power animal and using her fear and disgust (emotional arousal) as the force propelling her into trance."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as an outcome report, not as a sign announced in advance: a friend of Hine is said to have cured her spider phobia by deliberately taking the spider for her power animal. Reported at second hand, without duration or follow-up."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., Shape-Shifting, p. 37; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmp:ChaosMagicTmsWbwFearedCreatureAsPowerAnimal a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Cultivating a feared creature as power animal"@en, "Ein gefürchtetes Tier als Krafttier pflegen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Deliberately adopt a creature you fear as your power animal and use the fear and revulsion it arouses to drive yourself into trance."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., Shape-Shifting, p. 37; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwPhysicalIntensityGovernsTrance a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Physical intensity governs trance intensity"@en, "Körperliche Intensität bestimmt Tranceintensität"@de ;
    skos:definition "Visualising by itself is called the feeblest way to shape-shift; generally, it seems, the more physical the induction, the deeper the trance and the more 'real' the experience."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., Shape-Shifting, p. 37; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwShamansCursesStrikeSceptics a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Shamans' curses strike sceptics"@en, "Flüche von Schamanen treffen Skeptiker"@de ;
    skos:definition "There are said to be many cases of sceptical Westerners struck down by the curses of shamans, given in support of the Deep Mind's receptiveness to suggestion."@en ;
    skos:note "No case is named."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., Innerworld Exploration, p. 39; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmp:ChaosMagicTmsWbwGuidingClientThroughInnerworld a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Guiding a client through their innerworld"@en, "Einen Klienten durch seine Innenwelt führen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Help the client into trance and then guide them on their journey through their own innerworld, working with empathy and questions left open."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., Innerworld Exploration, p. 39; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwInnerworldGuidingYieldsSelfHealing a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Guiding a client's journey yields self-healing"@en, "Führung durch die Innenwelt führt zur Selbstheilung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Guiding a client through their innerworld is preferred because it lets the client find their own solution and draw on their own power to heal."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicTmsWbwGuidingClientThroughInnerworld ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwClientSelfHealingFromWithin ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., Innerworld Exploration, p. 39; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwClientSelfHealingFromWithin a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Client self-healing from power-from-within"@en, "Selbstheilung des Klienten aus innerer Kraft"@de ;
    skos:definition "The client is said to reach their own solution and draw on their own inner power to heal themselves; the guide mainly creates the space for this."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., Innerworld Exploration, p. 39; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmp:ChaosMagicTmsWbwConsultingAnAllyOnClientProblem a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Taking the problem to an ally in one's own innerworld"@en, "Das Problem zu einem Verbündeten in der eigenen Innenwelt tragen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Alternatively, bring the client's difficulty into your own innerworld and consult an ally there; conventional divination methods are also not to be overlooked."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., Innerworld Exploration, p. 39; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwWesternHeadTripVersusPhysicalShamanism a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Western magick as head-trip versus physical shamanism"@en, "Westliche Magie als Kopfsache gegenüber körperlichem Schamanismus"@de ;
    skos:definition "Taking up critics of Western magick who call it too abstract and mental, the author contrasts shamanism as thoroughly bodily, frequently to a painful degree."@en ;
    skos:note "The critics of Western magick are not named; only Hine's side is read."@en, "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicCondBWesternMagicTooCerebral (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 node holds Western magic too cerebral and offers relaxation and body awareness as the counter; here the contrast is with shamanism as thoroughly bodily, often to the point of real discomfort."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., Innerworld Exploration, p. 39; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwPainAndDisintegrationCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Pain, hallucination and near-disintegration"@en, "Schmerz, Halluzination und beinahe Zerfall"@de ;
    skos:definition "Shamanic magick is said often to involve pain, disordering of the senses, delusion, hallucination and conditions near obsession and disintegration of the personality (adding, ironically, that it is great fun)."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwWesternHeadTripVersusPhysicalShamanism ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Jack Your Body..., Innerworld Exploration, p. 39; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .



#
# Chemical Aids
#

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwDrugKnowledgeBeforeGuidingPrerequisite a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Knowledge of common drugs before practising"@en, "Kenntnis gängiger Drogen vor der Praxis"@de ;
    skos:definition "Even one who takes nothing stronger than mineral water is told it pays to know the common drugs, because sooner or later someone in trouble with a substance will arrive asking to be guided through it and can hardly be turned away."@en ;
    skos:note "Given as advice with a reason, not as a necessity with a named consequence."@en ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwBroadKnowledgeOfCommonDrugs ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwGuidingThroughABadTrip ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Chemical Aids, p. 40; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwBroadKnowledgeOfCommonDrugs a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Broad knowledge of common drugs"@en, "Breite Kenntnis gängiger Drogen"@de ;
    skos:definition "A working knowledge of the commoner street drugs and prescribed medicines, their effects, and what can be done for someone who has overdosed or is in a bad state."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Chemical Aids, p. 40; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwGuidingThroughABadTrip a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Guiding someone through a bad trip"@en, "Jemanden durch einen schlechten Trip begleiten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Helping a person who has taken more than they can handle through the experience."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Chemical Aids, p. 40; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwChemicalAidDefined a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Chemical aid defined; incense as chemical aid"@en, "Chemische Hilfe definiert; Räucherwerk als chemische Hilfe"@de ;
    skos:definition "Here any substance that assists entry into trance counts as a 'chemical aid'; society sanctions caffeine, alcohol and nicotine but not cannabis. Incense counts: beyond smell, some gums and resins raise skin temperature, others release carbon dioxide when burnt, and 'Tabunco' is said to cause strong drowsiness."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Chemical Aids, p. 40; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwCamphorCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Camphor affects the brain"@en, "Kampfer wirkt auf das Gehirn"@de ;
    skos:definition "Camphor is said to act on the brain and, in very large doses, to be able to cause epileptic fits."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwChemicalAidDefined ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Chemical Aids, p. 41; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwDirectedPerceptionBeforeDrugTrancePrerequisite a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Directed perception in drug trance"@en, "Gerichtete Wahrnehmung in der Drogentrance"@de ;
    skos:definition "Against the idea that one need only swallow a substance and passively let it happen: in trance one's perceiving has to be steered and concentrated."@en ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwDirectedPerceptionInTrance ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwChemicalAidDefined ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Passively letting the substance take effect gives no more than a good time, not the work of trance." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Chemical Aids, p. 41; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwDirectedPerceptionInTrance a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Directed perception in drug trance"@en, "Gerichtete Wahrnehmung in der Drogentrance"@de ;
    skos:definition "Keeping one's perception directed and focused during a trance brought on by a substance, instead of letting it simply happen."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Chemical Aids, p. 41; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmp:ChaosMagicTmsWbwGuidingApprenticesThroughDrugVisions a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Guiding apprentices through drug visions"@en, "Lehrlinge durch Drogenvisionen führen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Shamans are said often to train apprentices by leading them through their visions and teaching them how to placate the spirit of the substance used."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Chemical Aids, p. 41; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwGuidedVisionsYieldDrugSpiritTeaching a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Guided drug visions yield the spirits' teaching"@en, "Geführte Drogenvisionen eröffnen die Lehre der Geister"@de ;
    skos:definition "Apprentices guided through the visions and taught to appease the substance's spirit are those who, riding the spirits out, may learn much from them."@en ;
    skos:note "The text puts the training and the teaching in one passage and hedges the latter with it is said."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicTmsWbwGuidingApprenticesThroughDrugVisions ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwTeachingFromDrugSpirits ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Chemical Aids, p. 41; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwDrugSpiritsNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Spirits of drugs"@en, "Geister der Drogen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Every drug, plant-grown or in a capsule, is said to have its own spirit; the author's view is that these spirits are emergent properties of experience, called up like virtual particles from the interplay of the drug's molecules and the brain's neurochemical patterns, 'unbottled' when the substance hits the system. Some are friendly, some will try to kill you."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicCondBSpiritsPsychotropicsNaming (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 node names the spirits of psychotropics as testers who drive one to the edge of madness; here every drug, plant or capsule, has its spirit, which Hine likes to think of as an emergent property called up like a virtual particle between the drug's molecules and the brain's chemistry."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "spirit of the substance" ;
    pm:nameRole "spirit belonging to each drug, in Hine's view emergent from the meeting of drug and brain" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Chemical Aids, p. 41; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwTeachingFromDrugSpirits a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Teaching from drug spirits ridden out"@en, "Belehrung durch ausgestandene Drogengeister"@de ;
    skos:definition "If one rides the drug spirits out, they are said to be able to teach much."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Chemical Aids, p. 41; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwCarefulWithToxicSubstancesCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Be very careful with drug spirits and toxic substances"@en, "Größte Vorsicht bei Drogengeistern und giftigen Stoffen"@de ;
    skos:definition "One must be very careful: several reckless people are said to have died copying Carlos Castaneda by drinking psychotropic brews prepared by accommodating shamans; slipping up with any toxic substance is easy, and there is the legal side to consider."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwDrugSpiritsNaming ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Chemical Aids, p. 41; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwDyeCactusFailure a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The dye cactus"@en, "Der Färbekaktus"@de ;
    skos:definition "A friend and companions spent about eight hours brewing a cactus they had been told was hallucinogenic; after several cups, a local told them that two similar-looking cacti existed, one a strong vision-giver, the other used for dye, which taken internally is a powerful emetic."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: of a psychotropic brew made from a misidentified plant; after about eight hours of preparation a friend and his companions drank a cactus used for dye, which is a powerful emetic. No deadline or rate; the cause is put on mistaking two similar-looking cacti, and the story is reported at second hand."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Chemical Aids, p. 41; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .



#
# Going Down
#

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwUnderworldJourney a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Underworld Journey"@en, "Unterweltreise"@de ;
    skos:definition "The shaman is one who has been to the underworld and come back; the Underworld Journey is a willed going-down into the maze, holding on to the slenderest of threads. It is a recurring cycle of transformation with peaks, plateaux and troughs; change is a necessity of living and growing."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicCondCInitiationAsProcess (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 node defines initiation as a recurring threshold of change with peaks, troughs and plateaus whose key is recognition; here the shaman is defined as one who has been down and come back, the descent is a willed going into the labyrinth on the thinnest thread, and it comes to everyone and keeps calling."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Going Down, p. 42; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwDescentBrandedAsMadness a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Against culture's branding of the descent as madness"@en, "Gegen die kulturelle Brandmarkung des Abstiegs als Wahnsinn"@de ;
    skos:definition "Our culture is charged with leaving us unprepared, calling this process madness to be banished with needles and by the complacent psychologist who explains away dreams and desire for wildness, and with blunting the summons through those who limit imagination ('only imaginary', 'escapist'). The author reframes the shattering as breakthrough rather than breakdown."@en ;
    skos:note "Only Hine's side is read; the opponents are types (the psychologist, those who limit the imagination), not authors."@en, "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicCondCInitiatorySicknessLabelling (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 node rejects the psychiatric labelling of shamans as ethnocentric; here the charge is against the whole culture, which brands the descent as madness to be banished and blunts the summons, and the shattering is reframed as breakthrough."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Going Down, p. 42; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwKnowingTheSummonsSign a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Knowing the summons"@en, "Den Ruf erkennen"@de ;
    skos:definition "When the summons to the journey comes, one is told one will recognise it; the journey may take years and one will meet friends and allies."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a sign of success: of the summons to the underworld journey, which one will know when it comes; announced in advance, without a mark that would set it apart from any other crisis. The journey may take years."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Going Down, p. 42; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwNeverLeaveTheBoatCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Never leave the boat"@en, "Verlasse niemals das Boot"@de ;
    skos:definition "On the journey one will be challenged and sometimes crushed; stay in the boat unless you mean to go the whole distance; the trigger (crisis, drugs, gnosis, a near brush with death) is how allies open the doors, and the risk is going in feet first."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwUnderworldJourney ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Going Down, p. 43; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwDeathNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Death (personified)"@en, "Der Tod (personifiziert)"@de ;
    skos:definition "Death is spoken of as a female figure into whose arms one must surrender; she will most likely hand one back."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Death" ;
    pm:nameRole "a female figure into whose arms one surrenders and who will most likely hand one back" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Going Down, p. 43; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwDeathMayNotHandYouBackFailure a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Death may not hand you back"@en, "Der Tod gibt einen vielleicht nicht zurück"@de ;
    skos:definition "Surrendered to death, one will most likely be returned; if not, 'better luck next time'."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: of the surrender to death in the descent, which most likely returns one but may not; the failing case is answered only with 'better luck next time'. No deadline; the rate word is most likely; no cause is named."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Going Down, p. 43; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmp:ChaosMagicTmsWbwSurrenderAndPlummet a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Surrender and plummet"@en, "Hingabe und Absturz"@de ;
    skos:definition "When nothing else can be done, let go and give up control: picture yourself dangling from a branch over a cliff with no bottom, breathe in deeply, hope for the best and drop. If going down, go all the way; surrender to death."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicCondCSurrenderOfControl (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 node says that at some point control must be surrendered, which makes initiatory states hard to induce; here the surrender is given as an image to act on, letting go of the branch above a bottomless drop when nothing else can be done."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Going Down, p. 43; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwKnowingWhenNotToAct a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Knowing when not to act"@en, "Wissen, wann man nicht handelt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Knowing when to refrain from acting is called as valuable as knowing when to act; it comes only from attending to oneself and to what is happening around, with the body below the neck as a good ally."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Going Down, p. 43; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwAttentionBeforeKnowingWhenNotToActPrerequisite a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Self-attention before knowing when not to act"@en, "Selbstbeobachtung vor dem Wissen um Nichthandeln"@de ;
    skos:definition "This knowledge is gained only by paying attention to oneself and one's surroundings."@en ;
    skos:note "The prior step is attentiveness as already entered from Condensed Chaos. The text says this knowledge comes only by attention, but names no consequence beyond its absence."@en ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ChaosMagicCondAAttentiveness ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwKnowingWhenNotToAct ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Going Down, p. 43; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwManyNamesForTheDescent a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Many names for the descent"@en, "Viele Namen für den Abstieg"@de ;
    skos:definition "The process goes by several names, Nigredo, the Apophis phase, the Hump and Dark Night of the Soul, all said to name one thing: a time of testing into which one can carry nothing, neither beliefs, dogmas, weapons nor attachments."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Going Down, p. 43; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwAgainstInitiateElitism a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Against initiate elitism and secrecy"@en, "Gegen Eingeweihten-Elitismus und Geheimhaltung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author mocks those who refused to talk to him as a non-initiate or 'only a Neophyte', noting he found the withheld ritual details in a book by Farrar bought at a high-street shop; such grading is likened to newspaper snobbery."@en ;
    skos:note "Only Hine's side is read; the people mocked are not named, and the Farrar book is not named by title."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Going Down, p. 43; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwInitiationAsRecognisedTurningPoint a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Initiation as conscious recognition of a turning point"@en, "Einweihung als bewusstes Erkennen eines Wendepunkts"@de ;
    skos:definition "Initiation is defined as consciously recognising that one has reached a turning point in development and is entering a period of possible self-transformation; ritual is unnecessary though many find it a useful and enjoyable dramatisation; one can be initiated into a group's beliefs, but the deepest initiation is into oneself, and a guide can help but not do it for one."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicCondCThresholdRecognition (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 node makes recognition of the threshold the key to initiation; here the same definition is followed by the claims that ritual is unnecessary though useful as drama, that the deepest initiation is into oneself, and that a guide can help but not do it for one."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Going Down, p. 43; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwCatharsisTriggersThePlunge a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Catharsis as trigger for passing between worlds"@en, "Katharsis als Auslöser für den Übergang zwischen den Welten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Passing between the worlds is said usually to involve catharsis, some upheaval, ordeal or testing, which seems to trigger the plunge; it can arise from one's own developmental exercises, from a 'coincidental' event, or be induced by another."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Going Down, p. 44; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmp:ChaosMagicTmsWbwInitiatorsLightTouch a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The initiator's light touch"@en, "Die leichte Berührung des Initiators"@de ;
    skos:definition "As initiator, judge when to apply a light touch that triggers awareness of the initiation cycle in someone; touch and then let go, since once the person's active initiation begins there is little else to do except perhaps listen, or not, as the situation requires."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Going Down, p. 44; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwOutsiderPoseCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The outsider pose after initiation"@en, "Die Außenseiterpose nach der Einweihung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Initiation is likened to a mock death: many attachments will probably have to go and self-esteem drops on seeing one's own foolishness; the resulting outsider feeling, if clung to, quickly turns into a pose as chosen one, Magus or rebel. One must choose between staying in the void and reaching out to someone else."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicPrcAOutsiderGlamourCautela (Prime Chaos, 1999): the 1999 node lists the faults of the outsider self-image in general; here the outsider feeling is a product of initiation itself, which turns, if clung to, into the pose of the elect, the Magus or the rebel, with the choice between staying in the void and reaching out to another."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwInitiationAsRecognisedTurningPoint ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Going Down, p. 44; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwInitiatoryJourneyStages a pm:Sequencing ;
    rdfs:label "Stages of the initiatory journey"@en, "Stationen der Initiationsreise"@de ;
    skos:definition "The initiatory journey as Hine finds it told the world over, from fairy tales and plays to campfire stories and science fiction (his own favourites being the Jumping Mouse tale and the film Apocalypse Now), in eight stages from the summons to departure to the return to the community; he finds the same journey in the Tarot's major arcana."@en ;
    skos:note "No consequence of skipping a stage is named."@en, "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicCondCInitiatoryStages (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 node orders the initiatory process after Campbell into crisis and call, wilderness, initiatory sickness, dark night and further stages, as an editor-made order of the chapter; here the series is the eight-stage tale of the journey as told in myth and film, ending in a return to the community."@en ;
    rdf:_1 pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwJourneySummonsToDeparture ;
    rdf:_2 pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwJourneyRiverboat ;
    rdf:_3 pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwJourneyIntoChaos ;
    rdf:_4 pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwJourneyFriendsAndAllies ;
    rdf:_5 pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwJourneyTrialsAndObstacles ;
    rdf:_6 pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwJourneyAgentOfChange ;
    rdf:_7 pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwJourneyReturnToSurface ;
    rdf:_8 pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwJourneyReturnToCommunity ;
    pm:seriesStatement "Hine gives the stages in this order as the shape the journey tends to take: it begins with a summons and a vessel that carries one down, passes through chaos, allies, trials and a last meeting with an agent of change, and only then turns back to the surface and the community, where the role one returns to is left open." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Going Down, p. 44; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwJourneySummonsToDeparture a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Journey stage 1: Summons to departure"@en, "Reisestation 1: Ruf zum Aufbruch"@de ;
    skos:definition "The first stage of the initiatory journey: the call that sets one out."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Going Down, p. 44; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwJourneyRiverboat a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Journey stage 2: The riverboat"@en, "Reisestation 2: Das Flussboot"@de ;
    skos:definition "The second stage: the riverboat, the vessel that carries one down."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Going Down, p. 44; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwJourneyIntoChaos a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Journey stage 3: Journey into chaos"@en, "Reisestation 3: Reise ins Chaos"@de ;
    skos:definition "The third stage: the passage into disorder and the unknown."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Going Down, p. 44; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwJourneyFriendsAndAllies a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Journey stage 4: Meeting friends and temporary allies"@en, "Reisestation 4: Begegnung mit Freunden und zeitweiligen Verbündeten"@de ;
    skos:definition "The fourth stage: meeting friends and allies who help for a while."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Going Down, p. 44; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwJourneyTrialsAndObstacles a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Journey stage 5: Trials and obstacles"@en, "Reisestation 5: Prüfungen und Hindernisse"@de ;
    skos:definition "The fifth stage: ordeals and hindrances met on the way."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Walking Between the Worlds: Techniques of Modern Shamanism Volume One (Pagan News Publications, 1986; on-line version of December 1998), Going Down, p. 44; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item techniques-of-modern-shamanism-vol-1-2-and-3-by-phil-hine-collection, file Hine, Phil - Techniques of Modern Shamanism vol 1_djvu.txt" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTmsWbwJourneyAgentOfChange a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Journey stage 6: Last meeting with a bringer of Change"@en, "Reisestation 6: Letzte Begegnung mit einem Agenten des Wandels"@de ;
    skos:definition "The sixth stage: the final meeting, with an agent of Change."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:WalkingBetweenTheWorlds ;
